For each parameter, n_eff is a crude measure of effective sample size,
and Rhat is the potential scale reduction factor on split chains (at
convergence, Rhat=1).

---------------------------------

The fitted mu[1]/sigma[1] correspond to the simulated mu1/sigma1,
the fitted mu[2]/sigma[2] correspond to the simulated mu3/sigma3,
and the fitted mu[3]/sigma[3] correspond to simulated mu2/sigma2.
And the mixing proportions are estimated correctly.

Note that I only ran a single chain.  If you run multiple chains,
you'll find the indexes scrambling.  This model is not identifiable.
In fact, none of the parameters is identifiable.    The only thing
that will converge across chains is lp__.

If you need to assess convergence, you need to somehow re-identify
the model.  One way to do this is to sort.

You'd need to sort one of the parameters, like mu, then
apply the same sort order to the other parameters.  Then you
should be able to measure the convergence of the sorted parameters.
You want to choose a parameter that will sort properly, too.
If all the scaleses are the same, sorting on sigma won't work,
and if all the mixture proportions are the same, then sorting
on theta won't work.

We really need to add something like JAGS's sort in order to do
this easily within Stan.   It should be simple to add because
there are no gradients to compute for sort.

Ho long it takes to converge will depend on how good the
starting points are.  Random starting points seem to converge
in somewhere between a few dozen and few hundred iterations
depending on how close they are, and init=0 initializations
seem to converge reliably in a few dozen iterations.

- Bob

