I would like to further impose on the good-will of this group with one
further question---one which perhaps relates to the substance of my
modelling problem than the use of Stan. At the same time, I wouldn't put
 it past me to have made a basic error in trying to express my modelling ideas in code.

I think I have written a
 model which represents the substance of my problem. I gave budgetary
data observations nested in agencies, which are in turn nested within
agency types. Agency type is allowed to vary by year (and so hence do
the lower-levels which take a value according to agency type). The model
 (shown below) seems to converge pretty within about 10000 iterations.
The problem is that the credible intervals seem too narrow (shown in the
 graph below, which plots also the data). I get the concept of "shrinkage" in Bayesian models but this seems excessive! The variance of the sigmas is
pretty large, but if I make it any smaller, the estimates seem to "bump
up" against the upper limit of the sampling. In any case the variability
 in the data is pretty huge. When I set what I consider reasonable inits
 for a_type[k, 1], I don't seem to do any better than when I allow Stan
defaults to do the work.

So I guess the questions are (in
order): (1) am I doing something stupid, so that my code doesn't
represent the model I want to run? (2) is there anything I can do, like
changing priors? (3) Could it be that the sampler is not exploring the extremes of the distribution very effectively? On this last point, it doesn't seem to make a difference if I run a million iterations, and thin every 200 over the last 500000. It occurs to me that maybe the time-dependence
structure I am imposing by the t in 2:N_years loop may be too
restrictive, but I am not sure what alternatives to try.

The model:

hier_1 <- "data {
                int<lower=1> N_obs;
                int<lower=1> N_agencies;
                int<lower=1> N_years;
                int <lower=1, upper=N_agencies> agency[N_obs];
                int <lower=1, upper=N_years> year[N_obs];

                real <lower=0> y [N_obs];

                int <lower=1> N_types;
                int <lower=1, upper=N_types> type[N_agencies];

                }

            parameters {

                real <lower=0> a_agency[N_agencies, N_years];
                real <lower=0, upper=25000> sigma_y[N_agencies];
                real <lower=0> a_type[N_types, N_years];
                real <lower=0, upper=10000> sigma_a[N_types];
                real <lower=0, upper=10000> sigma_state[N_types];
                real <lower=0> mu_a_type1;
                real <lower=0, upper =100000> sd_a_type1;
                            }

            model {
                mu_a_type1 ~ normal (5000, 100);

