On Thursday, March 28, 2013 7:57:18 PM UTC-4, Tom Eagle wrote:
> 1) How should I change this to have multivariate normal parameters (b's)?

If you intend to have a diagonal covariance matrix, it is best to use (a product of) univariate normals.

> 2) Is there any way I can vectorize this simple example so I can learn how to write better code?

Here is how I would do it:

data {
   int<lower=1> n;         // number of obs
   int<lower=1> J;         // number of Rs
   int<lower=1> L;         // # of vars
   int<lower=1> id[n];     // Group ID (1, 2, 3, ..., nobs)  Must be sequenced 1,2,3,...
   int<lower=0> y[n];      // estimated counts (1 per obs)
   matrix[n,L] x;          // x matrix including intercept
}

parameters {

   vector[L] b[J];          // array of vectors
}

model {
   real log_eta[n];
   int id_i;

   // Priors

   for (j in 1:J) {

     b[j] ~ normal(0, 100);
   }

   // linear predictor

   for (i in 1:n) {

     id_i <- id[i]
     log_eta[i] <- b[id_i,1] * x[i,1] // if x[i,1] is always 1, don't multiply
                   + b[id_i,2] * x[i,2] + b[id_i,3] * x[i,3];
   }
   y ~ poisson_log(log_eta); // log-likelihood
}

The combination of this parameter declaration:

 > real<lower=7,upper=19> mu[K]; // locations of mixture components

and these priors:

 > mu[1] ~ normal(M[1],SD[1])  T[7,mu[2]-1.5];
 > mu[2] ~ normal(M[2],SD[2])  T[,mu[3]-1];
 > mu[3] ~ normal(M[3],SD[3])  T[,19];

is going to be problematic.  The support of the sampling
distribution for any variable should match the constraints
in its declaration.  If you don't initialize each mu[k]
in the truncated support, Stan can never move.  But even if
you do, you will run into significant rejection.

But I don't think you want a prior or structure in the model
to keep the means separated --- the data should do that.

Your real problem, though, is the data declaration

 > real y[N]; // observations

and sampling loop:

 > for (n in 1:N) {
 >   for (k in 1:K) {
 >     y[n] ~ normal(mu[k],sigma[k]);

This samples y[n] from each of the mu[k].  You need
to code as in this model:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/basic_estimators/normal_mixture.stan

